WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.179
Film Courage: I was watching a documentary
on Mary Shelley and it was

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:09.190
saying that she had to publish the story [Frankenstein]
anonymously (they didn’t think a woman could

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:15.430
write it) but also her age factored in and
I was wondering do you think that age factors

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.080
into creating meaningful work?

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:23.029
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: I don’t think age
is an issue if somebody has experienced something

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:25.900
that is worth communicating.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:32.119
I think that it is all based on what you have
inside of you at the time that you’re creating

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.910
stuff.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:39.410
So there are plenty of young people that have
had tremendously rich, dramatic even traumatic

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:46.450
experiences that are perfectly position to
do something magical with that life experience.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.880
I think the problem comes when you don’t
have enough life experience, you don’t have

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.890
anything to say.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.970
I can’t remember which filmmaker it was
who said when he graduated from film school

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.370
he said everybody was just scrambling to make
their first feature.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:08.670
He said “I’m going to take 5 years off
and I’m going to live.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.240
I don’t know but I’m going to live my
life.”

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.330
Because he even then knew that he didn’t
have anything to say that was worth saying

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:17.640
yet.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:25.069
So I think that’s probably a problem with
a lot of filmmakers is that if they don’t

00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:30.310
constantly inform their work with what’s
going on or if their lives aren’t enriched

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.119
by it (by certain experiences) that’s the
classic ‘Going Up Your Own A$$’ thing

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:39.740
where you’re not saying anything new, you’re
just repeating.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:45.719
So I think that is what is most important
is what is informing the work not necessarily

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:47.100
the age.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.999
And obviously Mary Shelley was perfectly in
a…had plenty to say on the subject and knocked

00:01:52.999 --> 00:01:54.509
it out so it didn’t really matter.

00:01:54.509 --> 00:02:01.409
I know Orsen Welles is obviously the prime
example of someone in his middle 20’s doing

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:09.869
the ultimate commentary on an old shattered
man’s life, that’s pretty amazing that

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:10.950
it would have that perspective.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.840
I guess it depends on the individual’s perspective.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.590
Film Courage: I think a lot of people think
that their life (and everyone’s life is

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.110
unique) but that “Oh I’ve had these tragedies.”

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.629
Even though one is a different set of tragedies
and the other is too, they are equally painful

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:27.250
to the person experiencing it [social problems
versus major life setbacks].

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:31.680
But don’t you think that everyone thinks
they have something to say at age 18 or 22?

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.860
Jack: Oh I think so.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:37.159
Film Courage: And some really do and maybe
some need to go out and…

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:38.159
Jack: I think so.

00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:39.159
I think it depends on the person.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:48.680
I think everybody thinks that their…everybody’s
life at any given moment is valid and traumatic.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:54.620
The question is does it translate into a movie.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.990
Obviously [Francois] Truffaut with 400 BLOWS
or even you look at Rob Reiner doing STAND

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:06.069
BY ME, if you nail it then you can paint a
picture of the drama and complexity of being

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:09.269
an adolescent.

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:13.370
That can be infinitely dramatic.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.090
So it’s not like a child’s life is invalid.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.740
Where I was just talking about THE RED BALLOON
the other day.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.430
THE RED BALLOON is one of those movies where
when I was in college everybody was like “Oh,

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.430
you’ve got to see THE RED BALLOON.”

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.159
And I was like “I don’t want to see a
movie about a little kid and his magical balloon.

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:26.439
That’s like who cares?

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.340
I want to see real gritty…”

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.569
And finally I sat down and finally I sat down
and watched THE RED BALLOON when I was like

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:40.580
21 and I’m [imitates crying] because it
perfectly captured that feeling of being 7

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:46.879
years old and alone and everybody hates you
(particularly if you are getting bullied)

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:52.409
and it just nailed…so it depends on the
story of the person who is telling it.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:56.760
Everybody’s life is valid, it just does
that translate into a 90-minute movie that

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:57.930
anybody is going to watch?

